WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.926 --> 00:00:05.046
history of squabbling over&nbsp;
their ambitions in space.

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:08.566
To understand the rivalry&nbsp;
we have to go back to 2004.

00:00:08.566 --> 00:00:10.566
When Musk and Bezos met for a dinner.

00:00:10.566 --> 00:00:13.446
According to Christian Davenport’s&nbsp;
book, the Space Barons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:17.846
Musk tried to give some tips to the&nbsp;
Amazon CEO about building rockets, saying:

00:00:17.846 --> 00:00:21.526
"I actually did my best to give good&nbsp;
advice, which he largely ignored.”

00:00:21.526 --> 00:00:24.246
Their space companies were&nbsp;
just start-ups at the time.

00:00:24.246 --> 00:00:27.926
Bezos - who has always been infatuated&nbsp;
with space - had the means to start Blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.926 --> 00:00:32.646
Origin in 2000 as he was flush with&nbsp;
cash from Amazon. Bezos is so keen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.646 --> 00:00:37.366
he recently stepped down as CEO of Amazon&nbsp;
to focus more attention on Blue Origin.

00:00:37.366 --> 00:00:40.966
Musk started SpaceX two years later with&nbsp;
the money he made when his company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.966 --> 00:00:43.286
sold to PayPal which was then bought by eBay.

00:00:43.286 --> 00:00:47.286
They spent the first decade building up&nbsp;
their ventures without a lot of bickering. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.286 --> 00:00:49.526
that all changed in 2013.

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:53.366
When SpaceX secured a long-term&nbsp;
lease of launchpad 39A at NASA’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.366 --> 00:00:57.126
Kennedy Space Center in Florida where&nbsp;
the Apollo moon missions took off from.

00:00:57.126 --> 00:00:59.286
Bezos wasn’t very happy about the deal.

00:00:59.286 --> 00:01:04.566
Blue Origin filed a formal protest with&nbsp;
NASA to prevent SpaceX from using it exclusively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.566 --> 00:01:09.286
arguing it should be turned into a “commercial&nbsp;
spaceport available to all launch companies."

00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:14.166
In reaction, Musk called the protest a "phony&nbsp;
blocking tactic" in an email to SpaceNews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:18.406
and said SpaceX would accommodate Blue Origin&nbsp;
should their vehicle be launch-ready. But he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.406 --> 00:01:22.646
couldn’t resist taking a swipe at Blue&nbsp;
Origin’s progress, writing “Frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.646 --> 00:01:27.126
I think we are more likely to discover&nbsp;
unicorns dancing in the flame duct.”

00:01:27.126 --> 00:01:30.726
SpaceX secured a 20-year lease&nbsp;
of the historic launch pad.

00:01:30.726 --> 00:01:33.846
The next year, it was SpaceX&nbsp;
that had beef with Blue Origin.

00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:37.206
In 2014, the U.S. Patent and&nbsp;
Trademark Office granted Blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.206 --> 00:01:40.726
Origin a patent for landing&nbsp;
rocket boosters on drone ships.

00:01:40.726 --> 00:01:44.246
This would have meant that SpaceX would&nbsp;
have to pay to use the technology.

00:01:44.246 --> 00:01:48.006
SpaceX argued that the concept of drone&nbsp;
ships had been around for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.006 --> 00:01:53.046
A judge sided with Musk’s firm, forcing Blue&nbsp;
Origin to withdraw most of its patent claims.

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:57.446
Since then, SpaceX has successfully landed&nbsp;
its Falcon 9 boosters on drone ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.446 --> 00:02:03.206
66 times to date - the first time was in April&nbsp;
2016 on the ship “Of course I still love you”.

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:08.006
As SpaceX achieved successful launch after&nbsp;
successful launch that year - Bezos tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.006 --> 00:02:12.806
to poach SpaceX’s President and COO Gwynne&nbsp;
Shotwell, according to the book Amazon Unbound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.806 --> 00:02:18.726
by Brad Stone. But Shotwell declined the job&nbsp;
of Blue Origin’s CEO. It would go to Bob Smith.

00:02:18.726 --> 00:02:23.046
Musk has had an issue with Blue Origin’s&nbsp;
hiring practices. He told Ashlee Vance who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.046 --> 00:02:27.766
wrote a biography about him that Blue Origin’s&nbsp;
tactic has been to snag talent away from SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.766 --> 00:02:32.326
by offering “double their salaries.” Adding,&nbsp;
“I think it’s unnecessary and a bit rude.”

00:02:32.326 --> 00:02:36.646
While SpaceX has sprinted ahead, Blue&nbsp;
Origin has progressed at a far slower pace.

00:02:36.646 --> 00:02:39.766
It successfully landed&nbsp;
New Shepard for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.766 --> 00:02:44.486
in 2015 - after which Bezos tweeted it was&nbsp;
“the rarest of beasts - a used rocket.”

00:02:44.486 --> 00:02:47.206
To which Musk pointed out it wasn’t exactly rare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.206 --> 00:02:51.766
SpaceX did the same with its prototype&nbsp;
Grasshopper three years before, in 2012.

00:02:51.766 --> 00:02:54.646
And Blue Origin still has not gone into orbit.

00:02:54.646 --> 00:02:58.566
It carried passengers for the first&nbsp;
time on a sub-orbital flight in July.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.566 --> 00:03:01.206
Bezos and three crewmates&nbsp;
flew past the Karman line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.206 --> 00:03:04.886
the internationally recognized boundary&nbsp;
of space, before returning to Earth.

00:03:04.886 --> 00:03:09.206
On the other hand, SpaceX has gone into&nbsp;
orbit with the Falcon 9 over 100 times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.206 --> 00:03:12.566
including taking astronauts to&nbsp;
the International Space Station.

00:03:12.566 --> 00:03:16.486
Both billionaires have their sights&nbsp;
set on sending people much farther.

00:03:16.486 --> 00:03:20.966
When SpaceX beat out Blue Origin to win a&nbsp;
coveted contract with NASA to land the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.966 --> 00:03:27.766
humans on the moon using this modified Starship,&nbsp;
Musk joked Bezos ‘can’t get it up’...to orbit!

00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:33.206
And speaking of sexual innuendos: Musk also poked&nbsp;
fun at the name of Blue Origin’s lunar lander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.206 --> 00:03:38.166
Blue Moon, tweeting: "Putting the word&nbsp;
'Blue' on a ball is questionable branding”.

00:03:38.166 --> 00:03:43.526
Blue Origin is suing NASA for awarding&nbsp;
SpaceX that contract worth $2.9 billion.

00:03:43.526 --> 00:03:48.326
The government agency originally planned on&nbsp;
investing in two lunar landers going forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.326 --> 00:03:52.806
The choices were SpaceX, Blue Origin,&nbsp;
and Dynetics. However, budget concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.806 --> 00:03:57.766
and a lack of funding from Congress scuttled&nbsp;
those plans – forcing NASA to choose just one.

00:03:57.766 --> 00:04:02.886
Blue Origin feels it’s dangerous choosing&nbsp;
only one company, writing in an open letter on its website:

00:04:02.886 --> 00:04:07.926
“Competition will prevent any single source&nbsp;
from having insurmountable leverage over NASA.”

00:04:07.926 --> 00:04:11.686
And created this infographic&nbsp;
blasting SpaceX’s&nbsp;Starship.

00:04:11.686 --> 00:04:16.326
The contract is only for an uncrewed test&nbsp;
flight and the first crewed landing on the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.326 --> 00:04:20.246
Blue Origin and others can still&nbsp;
bid on further contracts. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.246 --> 00:04:23.686
Blue Origin argues that SpaceX&nbsp;
now has a huge head start.

00:04:23.686 --> 00:04:28.646
And after this loss, it lost a lead engineer&nbsp;
who had been working on the lunar lander.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.646 --> 00:04:33.286
Nitin Arora announced in a LinkedIn&nbsp;
post he was moving over to SpaceX.

00:04:33.286 --> 00:04:36.006
Bezos’ other company Amazon also has a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.006 --> 00:04:39.046
with SpaceX’s satellite&nbsp;
internet service, Starlink.

00:04:39.046 --> 00:04:42.806
Over a thousand Starlink satellites have&nbsp;
already been launched, serving 90,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.806 --> 00:04:48.006
customers worldwide according to SpaceX. The&nbsp;
company aims to launch many thousands more.

00:04:48.006 --> 00:04:52.166
However, Amazon has urged the Federal&nbsp;
Communications Commission, the FCC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:56.806
to reject SpaceX’s updated plans that call&nbsp;
for two different satellite configurations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.806 --> 00:05:01.286
arguing SpaceX should have made these changes&nbsp;
“...before filing its application - not after.”

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:04.566
Why does Amazon care? Because it&nbsp;
plans to launch its own satellite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.566 --> 00:05:09.766
internet service dubbed Project Kuiper -&nbsp;
which led Musk to call Bezos a “copycat”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.766 --> 00:05:12.886
There’s no word on when Amazon’s&nbsp;
service will start operating.

00:05:12.886 --> 00:05:18.406
SpaceX responded to Amazon’s protest by calling it&nbsp;
“...a continuation of efforts by the Amazon family of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.406 --> 00:05:23.446
companies to hinder competitors to compensate for&nbsp;
Amazon’s failure to make progress of its own” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.446 --> 00:05:29.526
also noted that Amazon has “lodged objections to&nbsp;
SpaceX on average about every 16 days this year.”

00:05:29.526 --> 00:05:36.166
Musk then tweeted: “Filing legal actions against&nbsp;
SpaceX is *actually* (Bezos’) full-time job.”

00:05:36.166 --> 00:05:38.646
SpaceX is banking that its&nbsp;
high-speed internet service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.646 --> 00:05:43.286
will bring in billions of much-needed&nbsp;
dollars to fund the colonization of Mars.

00:05:43.286 --> 00:05:46.966
Settling on Mars is not an&nbsp;idea Bezos likes at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.966 --> 00:05:50.966
He’s made veiled comments about Musk’s&nbsp;
plans, once saying that Mount Everest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.966 --> 00:05:54.406
would be a “garden paradise” compared&nbsp;
to living on the freezing red planet.

00:05:54.406 --> 00:05:57.846
He also took a shot at one of the&nbsp;
reasons Musk wants to reside on Mars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.846 --> 00:05:59.606
In case Earth were to get destroyed.

00:05:59.606 --> 00:06:04.726
Bezos called this kind of backup plan “very&nbsp;
unmotivating” without naming the SpaceX CEO.

00:06:04.726 --> 00:06:08.326
On the other hand, Bezos envisions&nbsp;
a universe populated by floating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.326 --> 00:06:10.726
space colonies that hold a million people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.726 --> 00:06:15.446
some of which can resemble our own cities in order&nbsp;
to take the pressure off of Earth’s resources.

00:06:15.446 --> 00:06:19.286
But Blue Origin doesn’t have a timeline&nbsp;
to turn his vision into reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.286 --> 00:06:22.806
nor has it discussed how big of a role it&nbsp;
would play in trying to make this happen.

00:06:22.806 --> 00:06:27.046
Whereas SpaceX is operating with concrete&nbsp;
goals in mind. Musk has said he’s aiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.046 --> 00:06:32.166
to send people to the red planet for&nbsp;
the first time by 2026 on Starship.

00:06:32.166 --> 00:06:35.126
It all depends on the rate of&nbsp;
progress of the gigantic launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.126 --> 00:06:38.326
vehicle which is under development in south Texas.

00:06:38.326 --> 00:06:42.246
SpaceX conducted its first test flight&nbsp;
of the Starship in December 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.246 --> 00:06:46.006
a huge achievement that even Bezos&nbsp;
acknowledged, posting on Instagram:

00:06:46.006 --> 00:06:49.926
"Anybody who knows how hard this stuff&nbsp;
is impressed by today's Starship test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.926 --> 00:06:52.086
Big congrats to the whole @SpaceX team.&nbsp;
I'm confident they'll be back at it soon."

00:06:52.086 --> 00:06:55.446
Blue Origin is now working on&nbsp;
its own answer to Starship.

00:06:55.446 --> 00:06:58.966
New Glenn was originally designed&nbsp;
to have a reusable first stage but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.966 --> 00:07:03.526
not a reusable upper stage. However, the&nbsp;
company is reportedly working on making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.526 --> 00:07:08.806
the second stage reusable as well to compete&nbsp;
with Starship which is a fully reusable rocket.

00:07:08.806 --> 00:07:12.406
The secretive Blue Origin project&nbsp;
has been code-named “Jarvis”.

00:07:12.406 --> 00:07:16.006
When someone on Twitter suggested&nbsp;
that Blue Origin is acting like a kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.006 --> 00:07:19.926
copying someone’s homework, Musk&nbsp;
replied with laughing emojis.

00:07:19.926 --> 00:07:23.606
New Glenn was supposed to go on&nbsp;
its maiden voyage in late 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.606 --> 00:07:26.086
That has now been pushed to late 2022.

00:07:26.086 --> 00:07:32.166
Blue Origin’s motto is gradatim ferociter which&nbsp;
in Latin means “step by step, ferociously”.

00:07:32.166 --> 00:07:35.526
But at its pace so far, Musk&nbsp;
jokingly dismissed the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.526 --> 00:07:39.926
that Blue Origin was a true competitor in&nbsp;
an interview with the BBC a few years ago.

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:43.926
You’re obviously a very competitive person.&nbsp;
You’re competing with the likes of Jeff Bezos

00:07:45.606 --> 00:07:47.635
Jeff Who?

00:07:47.635 --> 00:07:50.646
And it's likely that this kind of banter will continue.

